  "anomaly_detected": true,
  "confidence": 0.7,
  "anomalies": [
    {
      "category": "wrong_value",
      "components": ["R6"],
      "pins": ["R6.1", "R6.2"],
      "nets": ["+5V", "REG_FB", "GND"],
      "observed": "On the Power sheet the MIC26903 feedback divider is R5 = 6k8 from the buck output (+5V node, after L1) to REG_FB, and R6 = 1M3 from REG_FB to GND. The sheet's own note gives Vout = 0.8*(1+R5/R6), which with these values yields 0.8*(1+6800/1300000) ≈ 0.80 V, not 5 V.",
      "expected": "The 5 V rail must regulate to 5 V (spec §4: 'MIC26903YJL-TR synchronous buck regulator steps the battery input down to 5 V'; schematic note '9A @ 5V max.'). With a 0.8 V reference and R5 = 6k8 as the top resistor, the bottom resistor R6 must be ≈ 1.3 kΩ (1k3) to satisfy Vout = 0.8*(1+R5/R6) ≈ 5 V. The drawn value 1M3 is three orders of magnitude too high.",
      "evidence": [
        {"source": "schematic", "quote": "Power sheet: R5 '6k8', R6 '1M3', note 'Vout=0.8*(1+R5/R6)' and '9A @ 5V max.'"},
        {"source": "spec", "quote": "§4.3: 'A MIC26903YJL-TR synchronous buck regulator steps the battery input down to 5 V ... feedback divider relation Vout=0.8*(1+R5/R6)'"}
      ],
      "impact": "The buck would regulate to ~0.8 V instead of 5 V, so the 5 V rail (servo/PWM power, GPS, CH330 USB-UART bridges) and the 3V3 LDO fed from it would collapse; the board would not power up correctly.",
      "confidence": 0.7
    }
  ],
  "summary": "The schematic is largely consistent with the specification (correct MCUs, sensors, INA219 at 0x40, PCA9685 at 0x41 to avoid bus collision, documented barometer pin-swap bug, etc.), but the MIC26903 feedback bottom resistor R6 is drawn as 1M3 instead of the ~1k3 required by the stated Vout=0.8*(1+R5/R6) for a 5 V rail, which would force the buck to ~0.8 V and kill the 5 V/3V3 supplies."
}

